# Code generated by Claude
"""
Offline search for the staging pose of the beaker-on-hotplate task: base (x, y, yaw), pedestal height and arm.

    source .venv/bin/activate
    python experiments/beaker_hotplate_staging_search.py

One process, no rendering, no physics stepping beyond a 1.4 s settle to read the base resting height.

Method
  1. Sample the arm's joint space: n_samples uniform joint vectors per arm (within the joint limits), run
     through a batch forward-kinematics of the arm chain (checked against MuJoCo's own FK to 1e-15 m) and keep
     the samples whose gripper pose is usable for a side pinch (SEARCH_CFG: approach axis forward and down, finger
     axis roughly horizontal). The TCP positions are stored in the openarm_body_link0 frame. The pedestal and the
     base only move that frame rigidly (it sits 0.75 + 2q above base_link, q = the lift command), so one sample
     set serves every base pose and pedestal height; the sampling is not repeated per candidate.
     (experiments/workspace_sweep.py was not reused: it is a physics-stepped sweep of 78k poses on the old
     single_block scene through the pre-position-actuator controller, far too slow for millions of samples, and
     its CSV predates the pedestal change.)
  2. Candidate grid: standoff (base front edge to bench edge) x lateral offset along the bench x pedestal command
     x arm, with the base facing the bench (yaw -90 deg). For every candidate the three world targets are moved into
     the body frame and the gap to the nearest reachable TCP sample is read from a KD-tree:
       grasp    beaker mid-height
       place    10 cm above hotplate_top
       ready    the TCP for the ready pose (above and in front of the beaker, ~10 cm clear)
     worst-case gap = max over the three (the ready target is added to the two asked for, since the arm has to
     get there at HANDOFF).
  3. Candidates are dropped if the footprint is closer than 5 cm to the bench top at the staging pose, or if the
     footprint swept by the final in-place turn (from the approach heading, i.e. the direction from the spawn, to
     the staging yaw, about base_link's origin) comes closer than 2 cm to the bench (the drive stops short of the
     goal, on the side away from the bench), or if the base comes within 3 cm of other lab furniture.
  4. Gaps below the sample resolution (1 cm) are ties. Ties are broken by how well the eagle camera sees the beaker
     and the hotplate from that pose (share of the image covered by the less visible of the two, from a ray grid, so
     occlusion counts; the camera is fixed to the torso, so it depends on the pedestal height),
     then by how many samples lie within 3 cm of the worst target (a candidate deep inside the workspace beats one
     on its edge), then by the larger standoff (more room for the 3 cm arrival tolerance).
  5. The winner is refined with damped-least-squares IK from the nearest samples, which gives the ready joint
     angles and an exact (IK) gap for each target, and the ready pose is checked in MuJoCo for contacts.
Results: data/beaker_hotplate_staging/candidates.csv, staging_result.json, and a config snippet on stdout.
"""

import csv
import json
import math
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path

import mujoco
import numpy as np
from scipy.spatial import cKDTree

PROJECT_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(PROJECT_ROOT))

from src import robot_specs  # noqa: E402
